Yes — if you terminate (delete) the EC2 instance, you stop being charged for the EC2 instance itself once it enters shutting-down/terminated.

But terminating the instance does not automatically mean every AWS resource is free. Check these:

| Resource                | After termination                                                     |
| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| EC2 compute             | ✅ No further instance charge                                          |
| Default EBS root volume | Usually deleted with instance if **Delete on termination** is enabled |
| Elastic IP              | ⚠️ Still charged until you **release** it                             |
| Snapshots               | ⚠️ Continue charging if you have any                                  |
| Other AWS resources     | ⚠️ Can continue charging                                              |


AWS currently charges public IPv4 addresses at $0.005/hour, including Elastic IPs; an allocated Elastic IP can remain billable even when it isn't attached to an instance.

