Yes. If your goal is “I don't want any AWS charges at all”, the safest approach is to delete/terminate resources you no longer need, not merely stop the EC2 instance.

AWS confirms that a stopped EC2 instance has no compute usage charge, but its EBS storage can still incur charges, and public/Elastic IPv4 addresses can also incur charges.

Do this for your project

1. Terminate the EC2 instance

Go to:

EC2 → Instances → select ckd_insurance → Instance state → Terminate (delete) instance

⚠️ This permanently deletes the instance. Your GitHub repository is safe, but the files on the EC2 disk may be deleted. AWS documents that the root EBS volume is deleted by default when its DeleteOnTermination setting is enabled.

2. Check Elastic IPs

Go to:

EC2 → Network & Security → Elastic IPs

If you allocated an Elastic IP and don't need it:

Select it → Actions → Release Elastic IP address

AWS charges for Elastic IP/public IPv4 addresses, including idle Elastic IPs.

3. Check EBS Volumes

Go to:

EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Volumes

After the instance is terminated, check whether any volume remains.

If there is an unattached volume that you don't need, delete it.

This is important because EBS storage can continue charging even when an EC2 instance is stopped.

4. Check Snapshots

Go to:

EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Snapshots

If you created snapshots for this project and don't need them, delete them.

5. Check other AWS services

Because you've been working with SageMaker and Secrets Manager, also check those services for resources you don't need.

Especially check:

SageMaker endpoints
SageMaker notebook/Jupyter resources
S3 buckets/objects
Secrets Manager secrets
NAT Gateways
Load Balancers
Finally, check Billing

Open the AWS Billing console and check Bills → Charges by service. AWS recommends reviewing charges by service and Region to find active resources.

For your situation

If you're finished with the course deployment today and your code is already on GitHub, I recommend:

Terminate EC2 → Release unused Elastic IP → Delete unused EBS volumes/snapshots → Check SageMaker → Check S3 → Check Billing.

That gives you the best chance of avoiding unexpected charges. Free Tier does not mean every AWS service/resource is automatically free, and Free Tier eligibility/credits have limits.

If you want to keep the project and use it again later, don't terminate yet—stopping preserves the EC2 disk, but you must accept possible EBS/IP charges.

